://www.researchgate.net/publication/407654982_Extremal_Spanning_Trees_in_Product_Grid_Graphs)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs ... We study how fixed-volume spanning-tree extremality changes when product-grid boundary factors are free, periodic, or mixed. ... denote the number of spanning trees of a ﬁnite connected graph ... The broader periodic conjecture should be viewed more cautiously than Conjecture 9.1:\n\n# Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs\n\n  * June 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.24016\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nJiechen Zhang\n\nJiechen Zhang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study how fixed-volume spanning-tree extremality changes when product-grid boundary factors are free, periodic, or mixed. In two dimensions, extremality depends sharply on the boundary type. The free/free and periodic/periodic products both obey a closest-to-square principle: among fixed-area rectangles, $P_r\\square P_s$ and $C_r\\square C_s$ are maximized by the closest-to-square admissible side lengths. The mixed free/periodic cylinder $P_r\\square C_s$ is different: closest-to-square fails, and in the divisor-rich case the optimizing cyclic circumference has scale $N^{1/3}$ when the area is N=rs. \nprinciple: among ﬁxed-area rectangles,\n\n𝑃 𝑟□𝑃 𝑠\n\nand\n\n𝐶 𝑟□𝐶 𝑠\n\nare maximized by the closest-to-square\n\nadmissible side lengths. The mixed free/periodic cylinder\n\n𝑃 𝑟□𝐶 𝑠\n\nis diﬀerent: closest-to-square\n\nfails, and in the divisor-rich case the optimizing cyclic circumference has scale\n\n𝑁 1/3\n\nwhen the\n\narea is\n\n𝑁=𝑟𝑠\n\n. In arbitrary dimension we prove pairwise balancing theorems for pure free\n\nproducts and pure periodic products, and then strengthen them by a heat-trace Schur-concavity\n\ntheorem in logarithmic side lengths. At perfect-power volume this gives the unique maximizers\n\n𝑃□𝑑\n\n𝑛\n\nand, for\n\n𝑛≥3\n\n,\n\n𝐶□𝑑\n\n𝑛\n\n. These product-grid comparisons motivate perfect-power conjectures\n\nfor connected induced lattice subgraphs and periodic analogues.\n\n‗EPFL. Email: jiechen.zhang@epﬂ.ch.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2606.24016v1 [math.CO] 22 Jun 2026\n\n1 Introduction\n\nLet\n\n𝜏(𝐺)\n\ndenote the number of spanning trees of a ﬁnite connected graph\n\n𝐺\n\n. Throughout, a path\n\nfactor\n\n𝑃 𝑟\n\nis a free boundary factor, while a cycle factor\n\n𝐶 𝑟\n\nis a periodic boundary factor. Thus\n\n𝑃 𝑟□𝑃 𝑠\n\nis the basic rectangular grid,\n\n𝐶 𝑟□𝐶 𝑠\n\nis the torus, and\n\n𝑃 𝑟□𝐶 𝑠\n\nis the cylinder. The central question is:\n\namong product grids of ﬁxed volume, which side lengths maximize\n\n𝜏\n\n, and how does the answer\n\ndepend on the boundary factors?\n\nThe answer is boundary-sensitive even in two dimensions. The free/free case was settled\n\nquantitatively by the rectangular-grid balancing theorem of Zhang [\n\nZha26\n\n]: among equal-area\n\nrectangular grids, moving the side lengths toward a square gives a sharp linear lower bound for the\n\n\nFoltz recorded a two-dimensional square-maximality conjecture for connected induced grid\n\nsubgraphs [\n\nPTF22\n\n]; the product-extremal conjectures in Section 9 are higher-dimensional product-\n\ngrid analogues. Tapp’s boundary-sensitive bounds for square-lattice subgraphs give broader\n\nextremal context for how boundary size aﬀects spanning-tree counts [\n\nTap24\n\n], but they do not imply\n\nthe exact ﬁnite product-grid comparisons proved here.\n\nWe use standard majorization terminology, including Karamata’s inequality and Schur-\n\nconvexity/concavity [MOA11]; in Section 8 it is applied to logarithmic side lengths.\n\nOrganization. Section 2 records spectra, Matrix–Tree normalization, and the one-dimensional\n\npolynomial identities. Section 3 derives the product formulas for rectangles, cylinders, and\n\ntori. Section 4 proves the trapezoidal identities and monotonicity estimates used in the periodic\n\narguments. Section 5 treats the two symmetric boundary cases. Section 6 proves the cylinder\n\nnormal form, counterexamples to square balancing, bounded-aspect asymptotics, and the\n\n𝑁 1/3\n\ncircumference result. Section 7 proves the massive determinant inequalities. Section 8 applies them\n\n3\n\nto pure products and proves Schur-concavity and the perfect-power result by heat traces. Section 9\n\ndiscusses the scope of the methods, mixed boundary products, and product-extremal conjectures.\n\n2 Preliminaries\n\nFor\n\n𝑟≥1\n\ndeﬁne\n\n𝜆 𝑃\n\n𝑖(𝑟)=2−2 cos(𝜋 𝑖/𝑟)\n\nfor\n\n0≤𝑖≤𝑟−1\n\n, and for\n\n𝑟≥3\n\ndeﬁne\n\n𝜆 𝐶\n\n𝑖(𝑟)=2−2 cos(2 𝜋 𝑖/𝑟)\n\nfor\n\n0≤𝑖≤𝑟−1\n\n. These are the Laplacian spectra of\n\n𝑃 𝑟\n\nand\n\n𝐶 𝑟\n\n, respectively. The Laplacian spectrum\n\nof a Cartesian product is the multiset of all sums of eigenvalues from the factors. Kirchhoﬀ’s\n\nMatrix–Tree Theorem [Kir47] gives\n\n|𝑉(𝐺)|𝜏(𝐺)=\n\n𝜆∈Spec+(𝐺)\n\nProof.\n\nWe give the proof simultaneously for\n\n𝑋=𝑃\n\nand\n\n𝑋=𝐶\n\n, using the cycle hypotheses in the\n\nperiodic case. Fix 𝑡>0. By Lemmas 8.9 and 8.10, the function\n\n𝑓 𝑡(𝑢)=log ℋ 𝑋(𝑒 𝑢, 𝑡)\n\nis strictly convex and agrees with\n\nlog ℎ 𝑋(𝑚, 𝑡)\n\nat admissible integer side lengths. Since\n\n(log 𝑛 𝑖)\n\nmajorizes (log 𝑚 𝑖), Karamata’s inequality gives\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\nlog ℎ 𝑋(𝑛 𝑖, 𝑡)=\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\n𝑓 𝑡(log 𝑛 𝑖) ≥\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\n𝑓 𝑡(log 𝑚 𝑖)=\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\nlog ℎ 𝑋(𝑚 𝑖, 𝑡).\n\nThe inequality is strict unless the logarithmic side-length tuples agree up to permutation. Therefore\n\nTr(𝑒−𝑡𝐿(𝑋 𝑛 1□···□𝑋 𝑛 𝑑))=\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\nℎ 𝑋(𝑛 𝑖, 𝑡) ≥\n\n𝑑\n\n\n\n𝑖=1\n\nℎ 𝑋(𝑚 𝑖, 𝑡)=Tr(𝑒−𝑡𝐿(𝑋 𝑚 1□···□𝑋 𝑚 𝑑)),\n\nstrictly for every 𝑡>0 unless the tuples agree up to permutation.\n\nApply Lemma 8.7 with\n\n𝐺=𝑋 𝑛 1□···□𝑋 𝑛 𝑑\n\nthen\n\n𝜏(ℒ 𝑑[𝑆]) ≤ 𝜏(𝑃□𝑑\n\n𝑛).\n\nEquality should occur only for the\n\n𝑑\n\n-dimensional box\n\n𝑃□𝑑\n\n𝑛\n\n, up to lattice translation and coordinate permutation.\n\nFor\n\n𝑑=2\n\nthis specializes to the square-maximality conjecture recorded by Procaccia and\n\nTucker-Foltz [\n\nPTF22\n\n]. For non-perfect-power\n\n𝑁\n\n, one still expects a maximizing induced grid\n\nsubgraph to be a compact near-box shape, but the correct ﬁnite shape may be non-product. Tapp’s\n\nboundary-sensitive estimates support this compactness principle, but the product-grid comparisons\n\nhere show that exact spanning-tree maximality requires more than edge-count or boundary-size\n\ncontrol.\n\nThere is also a natural periodic analogue, although the correct ambient class is less canonical\n\nthan connected induced subgraphs of\n\nZ 𝑑\n\n. One ﬁnite-quotient formulation is the following. Let\n\nΛ⊂Z 𝑑\n\nbe a full-rank sublattice, let\n\n𝑆⊂Z 𝑑\n\nbe\n\nΛ\n\n-periodic, and let\n\nℒ 𝑑[𝑆]/Λ\n\nbe the corresponding\n\nﬁnite simple quotient graph. This includes rectangular tori\n\n𝐶 𝑚 1□···□𝐶 𝑚 𝑑\n\nby taking\n\n𝑆=Z 𝑑\n\nand\n\nΛ = 𝑚 1 Z 𝑒 1⊕ · ·· ⊕ 𝑚 𝑑 Z 𝑒 𝑑.\n\nConjecture 9.2. Let\n\n𝑑≥2\n\nand\n\n𝑛≥3\n\n. Among connected quotient graphs\n\nℒ 𝑑[𝑆]/Λ\n\nwith\n\n𝑛 𝑑\n\nvertex orbits,\n\nthe spanning-tree count is at most\n\n𝜏(𝐶□𝑑\n\n𝑛).\n\nEquality should occur only for the standard product torus 𝐶□𝑑\n\n𝑛, up to the natural quotient symmetries.\n\nFor general periodic volumes, the right ﬁnite benchmark is left open. Product tori remain\n\nthe natural comparison class, and inside that subclass Theorem 8.11 identiﬁes the most balanced\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRefutation of the unrestricted strong distance-two period bound | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/demo-2026-strong-period-bound/)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Gregory Demo, David Lund and Oleksiy Al-saadi, On the Periodicity of k-distance Graphs, arXiv:2606.25493v1, Section 4, Conjecture 1, printed PDF page 11: ... Let $V$ be a finite vertex set and $G$ a simple undirected graph on $V$.\n\n# Refutation of the unrestricted strong distance-two period bound\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/Graph/Walks/DemoStrongPeriodBoundRefutation.resulthttps://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25493v1Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nGregory Demo, David Lund and Oleksiy Al-saadi, On the Periodicity of k-distance Graphs, arXiv:2606.25493v1, Section 4, Conjecture 1, printed PDF page 11:\n\n> Conjecture 1. Any connected graph G=(V,E) has ρ²(G)≤|V|−3.\n\nThe exact target and integer interpretation are registered in #23003. The source note records the source qualification; the earlier metamour note records the known fixed-point example.\n\n### Definitions and statement echo\n\nLet $V$ be a finite vertex set and $G$ a simple undirected graph on $V$. Define $T(G)$ on the same vertices by $u\\sim_{T(G)}v\\iff\\operatorname{edist}_G(u,v)=2$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2606.24016v2)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; ## Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs ... graph spanning tree theta function tree permutation representation\n\n## Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs\n\nJiechen Zhang\n\nPublished: 2026-09-27 | Updated: 2026-09-29\n\nComments: 25 pages\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ncs.DM math.CO math.SP\n\ngraph spanning tree theta function tree permutation representation\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe compare spanning-tree counts at a fixed number of vertices for Cartesian products of paths $P_r$, $r\\ge1$, or cycles $C_s$, $s\\ge3$. With the number $d$ of factors fixed, an equal-volume tuple that is more balanced in the logarithmic majorization order has at least as many spanning trees, with equality only for permutations. This comparison persists after adjoining any fixed finite connected Cartesian factor; equal-sided blocks therefore uniquely maximize the count at admissible block volume $n^d$. We prove the comparison through strict log-convexity of one-dimensional heat traces in logarithmic length. The Hartman--Watson representation expresses the cycle interpolation as a positive mixture of theta functions, and the path case follows from it. The same heat-trace order decreases every positive inverse-power sum of the nonzero Laplacian eigenvalues, and hence the total effective resistance. Cylinders behave differently: square balancing can fail. For $P_r\\square C_s$ of area $N=rs$, extending the product formula to real height and optimizing over integer circumferences $3\\le s\\le N$ yields an optimum on the scale $N^{1/3}$. Along areas having a divisor asymptotic to that optimum, quantitative estimates place every graph-maximizing circumference on the same scale. For two-dimensional tori, balancing remains optimal, but the gain in the logarithmic spanning-tree count admits no positive uniform lower bound proportional to the decrease in the side sum.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202602214972623958)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs ...   *   * Copyright (c) 2009 Japan Science and Technology Agency.\n\nPreprint\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202602214972623958   Reference number：26P0206500\n\n# Extremal Spanning Trees in Product Grid Graphs\n\nAuthor (1)：\n\nZhang Jiechen\n\n\nMaterial：\n\narXiv\n\nAbout arXiv\n\n  * Search \"O7000B\"\n\nPublication year： Jun. 22, 2026  update date on preprint server： Jun. 24, 2026\nJST Material Number： O7000B  Document ty